# pyenv
#   For a library or package, you might want to ignore these files since the
#   code is intended to run in multiple environments; otherwise, please comment
#   out to track and install in your environment:
# .python-version

# pipenv
#   According to pypa/pipenv#598, it is recommended to include Pipfile.lock in version control.
#   However, in collaboration, if you have platform-specific requirements or
#   dependencies without cross-platform support, you might want to delete
#   this to maintain consistency while developing:
# Pipfile.lock

# UV
#   Similar to Pipfile.lock, it is recommended to include uv.lock in version control.
#   This is especially recommended for binary packages to ensure reproducible
#   builds when distributing the package.
# uv.lock

# poetry
#   Similar to Pipfile.lock, it is recommended to include poetry.lock in version control.
#   https://python-poetry.org/docs/basic-usage/#commit-your-poetrylock-file-to-version-control
# poetry.lock
# poetry.toml

# pdm
#   Similar to Pipfile.lock, it is recommended to include pdm.lock in version control.
#   pdm recommends storing project-wide configuration in pdm.toml, but excluding .pdm-python.
#   https://pdm-project.org/en/latest/usage/#project/#working-with-project/
# pdm.lock
# pdm.toml
.pdm-python
.pdm-build/

# pixi
#   Pixi creates a virtual environment in the .pixi directory, just like venv module creates
#   one in the .venv directory. It is not recommended to include this directory in version control.
.pixi

# PEP 582; used by e.g. github.com/David-OConnor/pyflow and github.com/pdm-project
